  overview: 'This topic explains how metals are ranked and extracted, how acids react to make salts, and how electricity can break down ionic compounds. It includes two AQA required practicals: <b>making a soluble salt</b> and <b>electrolysis of aqueous solutions</b>.',
  objectives: ['I can use the reactivity series to explain displacement and extraction', 'I can identify oxidation and reduction (HT: in terms of electrons)', 'I can predict the products of acid reactions and name salts', 'I can describe how to make a pure, dry soluble salt', 'I can predict the products of electrolysis of molten and aqueous compounds', 'I can explain strong/weak acids and pH (HT), and titrations (chemistry only)'],
  keyWords: [{ term: 'Oxidation', def: 'Gain of oxygen or (HT) loss of electrons.' }, { term: 'Reduction', def: 'Loss of oxygen or (HT) gain of electrons.' }, { term: 'Ore', def: 'Rock containing enough metal to be worth extracting.' }, { term: 'Electrolyte', def: 'Molten or dissolved ionic compound that conducts.' }, { term: 'Cathode', def: 'Negative electrode; cations gain electrons here.' }, { term: 'Anode', def: 'Positive electrode; anions lose electrons here.' }, { term: 'Strong acid', def: 'Completely ionised in water.' }, { term: 'Weak acid', def: 'Only partially ionised in water.' }],
  sections: [
    { title: 'Reactivity and extraction', html: `<p>Reactivity series: K, Na, Li, Ca, Mg, Al, <b>C</b>, Zn, Fe, <b>H</b>, Cu, Ag, Au.</p><ul><li>Below carbon → reduce oxide with carbon (iron in a blast furnace).</li><li>Above carbon → electrolysis (aluminium).</li></ul><div class="rule">OIL RIG (HT): Oxidation Is Loss of electrons, Reduction Is Gain</div><p>Ionic equation for displacement: Fe + Cu<sup>2+</sup> → Fe<sup>2+</sup> + Cu (sulfate ions are spectators).</p>` },
    { title: 'Making a soluble salt (required practical)', html: `<ol><li>Warm dilute acid.</li><li>Add insoluble base (oxide/carbonate) until in excess.</li><li>Filter off excess.</li><li>Evaporate some water on a water bath, then leave to crystallise.</li><li>Pat crystals dry.</li></ol><div class="worked"><div class="wq">Name the reactants to make zinc chloride.</div><ol><li>Chloride → hydrochloric acid</li><li>Zinc → zinc oxide or zinc carbonate (insoluble base)</li></ol><div class="wa">Answer: zinc oxide + hydrochloric acid → zinc chloride + water</div></div>` },
    { title: 'Electrolysis', html: `<p>Cations → cathode (−), gain electrons (reduction). Anions → anode (+), lose electrons (oxidation).</p><table class="qt"><tr><th>Aqueous solutions</th><th>Rule</th></tr><tr><td>Cathode</td><td>Metal forms if less reactive than hydrogen; otherwise <b>hydrogen</b></td></tr><tr><td>Anode</td><td>Halogen forms if halide present; otherwise <b>oxygen</b></td></tr></table><p>Aluminium: Al<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub> dissolved in cryolite; Al at cathode; O<sub>2</sub> at carbon anodes, which burn away.</p><p>HT half equations: Cu<sup>2+</sup> + 2e<sup>−</sup> → Cu; 2Cl<sup>−</sup> → Cl<sub>2</sub> + 2e<sup>−</sup>; 4OH<sup>−</sup> → O<sub>2</sub> + 2H<sub>2</sub>O + 4e<sup>−</sup>.</p>` },
    { title: 'Strong and weak acids', html: `<p>Strong acids (HCl, HNO<sub>3</sub>, H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>) are fully ionised; weak acids (ethanoic, citric, carbonic) partly ionised.</p><div class="rule">HT: pH falls by 1 → H<sup>+</sup> concentration × 10</div><p>Chemistry only — titrations: pipette 25.0 cm³ alkali, add indicator, add acid from burette until colour changes, repeat for concordant results.</p>` },
  ],
  mistakes: ['Saying sodium forms at the cathode in aqueous sodium chloride — hydrogen does.', 'Confusing strong with concentrated.', 'Writing half equations with electrons on the wrong side.', 'Heating salt solutions to dryness in the salt practical.'],
  examTips: ['Learn the aqueous electrolysis rules — they appear in 4–6 mark questions.', 'For required practical questions, name equipment and give reasons for each step.', 'In redox questions, say what is oxidised AND what is reduced.'],
  realLife: 'Every aluminium drinks can started as bauxite ore, electrolysed in huge cells using as much electricity as a small town.',
